SGLT2-Inhibitor-Induced Euglycemic Diabetic Ketoacidosis (eDKA).

### Patient Demographics
* Identifier: Patient N.D.
* Age: 51 years
* Gender: Female
* Weight: 64 kg
* Medical History: Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (6 years), Hypertension, ongoing low-carbohydrate dietary regimen.

### Clinical Chronology
* Month 1 (Day 1): Added Empagliflozin 10 mg orally once daily to existing Metformin therapy to improve glycemic control.
* Month 2 (Day 10): Patient developed mild gastroenteritis with reduced oral intake.
* Month 2 (Day 12): Patient presented to the emergency department with severe nausea, persistent vomiting, generalized abdominal pain, tachypnea, and fatigue.
* Laboratory Findings at Admission:
  * Blood Glucose: 168 mg/dL (Euglycemic; under 250 mg/dL)
  * Arterial Blood pH: 7.18 (Normal reference range: 7.35 to 7.45; severe metabolic acidosis)
  * Serum Bicarbonate: 11 mEq/L (Normal reference range: 22 to 29 mEq/L)
  * Anion Gap: 22 mEq/L (Elevated; reference range: 8 to 12 mEq/L)
  * Serum Beta-Hydroxybutyrate: 5.4 mmol/L (Elevated; reference range: under 0.5 mmol/L)
  * Urinalysis: Strongly positive for ketones, positive for glucose.
* Intervention: Immediate permanent discontinuation of Empagliflozin; patient admitted to the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) for IV insulin infusion, continuous dextrose infusion, and aggressive fluid/electrolyte management.
* Follow-up (Day 5 post-discontinuation): Complete resolution of ketoacidosis, normalization of arterial pH (7.41) and anion gap; patient transitioned to basal-bolus insulin regimen.

